title: What a client saw, during both deployments
subtitle: watch_shift.sh against the ALB, 2026-09-29 - polling every 2s
width: 1050
---
# DEPLOYMENT 1 -- v1 to v2, a normal release.

  Responses seen
    v1                       62
    v2                       86

  [ok]     traffic moved between versions during the window -- 2 distinct responses
  [ok]     the shift became visible to a client at t+182s

  passed 2   failed 0   BROKEN 0

# No failed request, and no mix. With one task and the plain BLUE_GREEN
# strategy the listener rule simply switches: every request after t+182s
# got v2. A percentage split needs the canary or linear strategy and more
# than one task.


# DEPLOYMENT 2 -- v3, deliberately broken. No document root, so / is a 404.

  Responses seen
    v2                       214

  [ok]     one version served throughout (214 requests) -- steady state

  passed 1   failed 0   BROKEN 0

# 214 requests. Every one served by the OLD version. Not one client saw
# the broken release.
#
# And the alarm that exists to catch this never fired:
#
#   aws cloudwatch describe-alarm-history --alarm-name week21-bluegreen-target-4xx
#     (no state change during the deployment)
#
# The target group rejected the new tasks before they ever received
# traffic -- "Health checks failed with these codes: [404]" -- so there
# were no client 4xx to alarm on.
#
# The users were protected. Just not by the mechanism built to protect them.
